e paper presents this as an analogue for future research after disproving the corresponding $\\bar{X}$X ˉ-coloring conjecture; no resolution is given here.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nJorik Jooken, “A note on X-coloring and A-coloring 4-regular graphs”, arXiv:2401.05510 (2024).\n\ncubic-graphsgraph-coloringline-graphs\n\n0 0\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2510.19164] Partition functions that repel perfect-powers (https://slimjectvitamins.com/accel-vitamin-b-complex-capsules/?_=%2Fabs%2F2510.19164%23CD3DgejCa2b3OIySVEszRQc%3D)\nciteturn4search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: last month; View a PDF of the paper titled Partition functions that repel perfect-powers, by Ken Ono ... > Abstract:A conjecture by Sun states that the partition function $p(n)$, for $n>1$, is never a perfect power. ... Cite as:  | arXiv:2510.19164 [math.NT]\n\n# Mathematics > Number Theory\n\n[Submitted on 22 Oct 2025 (v1), last revised 24 Oct 2025 (this version, v2)]\n\n# Title:Partition functions that repel perfect-powers\n\nAuthors:Ken Ono\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Partition functions that repel perfect-powers, by Ken Ono\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:A conjecture by Sun states that the partition function $p(n)$, for $n>1$, is never a perfect power. Recent work by Merca et al. proposes generalizations of perfect-power repulsion for $p(n)$. In this note, we prove these generalizations for the functions $p_B(n)$, which count the number of partitions of $n$ with the largest part $\\leq B$. If $B\\geq 4$ and $k\\geq 3$, with $k\\nmid (B-1)$, then we prove that there are only finitely many pairs $(n,m)$ for which $$\\lvert p_B(n)-m^k\\rvert\\le d.$$ These results support Sun and Merca et al.'s conjectures, as $p_B(n) \\rightarrow p(n)$ when $B \\rightarrow +\\infty.$ To prove this, we reduce the problem to Siegel's Theorem, which guarantees the finiteness of integral points on curves with genus $\\geq 1$.\n\nComments:  | For the Academy of Romanian Scientists. In fact to appear in Annals of Acad. Rom. Sci. This version includes one new reference and one new remark\nSubjects:  | Number Theory (math.NT); Combinatorics (math.CO)\nMSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 11P82, 05A17, 05A20\nCite as:  | arXiv:2510.19164 [math.NT]\n   | (or arXiv:2510.19164v2 [math.NT] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.19164 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Ken Ono [view email]\n[v1] Wed, 22 Oct 2025 01:48:41 UTC (10 KB)\n[v2] Fri, 24 Oct 2025 00:49:07 UTC (10 KB)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLeader–Russell–Walters' transitive-set conjecture for Euclidean Ramsey sets | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/371018/leader-russell-walters-transitive-set-conjecture-for-euclide)\nciteturn4search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Leader–Russell–Walters' transitive-set conjecture for Euclidean Ramsey sets ... Natalie Behague, “Nearly all known Euclidean Ramsey sets are subsoluble”, arXiv:2510.15677 (2025).\n\n# Leader–Russell–Walters' transitive-set conjecture for Euclidean Ramsey sets\n\nAbout 1 year old · traced to 2025\n\nA finite set $X\\subset\\mathbb{R}^d$X⊂R d is Ramsey if, for every $k$k, there exists an integer $n$n such that every $k$k-colouring of $\\mathbb{R}^n$R n contains a monochromatic isometric copy of $X$X. A finite set is transitive if a group acts transitively on it; a set is isometric to a subset of a finite transitive set when some finite transitive Euclidean set contains an isometric copy of it. Leader–Russell–Walters' conjecture. A set $X\\subset\\mathbb{R}^d$X⊂R d is Ramsey if and only if it is isometric to a subset of a finite transitive set. This conjecture is presented as a rival to Graham's conjecture and concerns whether the known Ramsey constructions account for all Ramsey sets. The paper investigates the converse relationship with subsolubility and reports that only two possible exceptions remain among nearly all known examples.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nNatalie Behague, “Nearly all known Euclidean Ramsey sets are subsoluble”, arXiv:2510.15677 (2025).\n\ncombinatoricseuclidean-ramsey-theoryramsey-theory\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2025-10-28&page=177&range=31)\nciteturn4search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: yesterday;   * Nearly all known Euclidean Ramsey sets are subsoluble ... Oct 20 2025 math.CO arXiv:2510.15677v3\n\n  * Nearly all known Euclidean Ramsey sets are subsoluble\n\nNatalie Behague\n\nOct 20 2025 math.CO arXiv:2510.15677v3\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2510.15677, author = {Natalie Behague}, title = {{N}early all known {E}uclidean {R}amsey sets are subsoluble}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2510.15677}, note = {arXiv:2510.15677v3} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nA finite set $X$ in a Euclidean space $\\mathbb{R}^d$ is called Ramsey if for every $k$ there exists an integer $n$ such that whenever $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is coloured with $k$ colours, there is a monochromatic copy of $X$. Graham conjectured that all spherical sets are Ramsey, but progress on this conjecture has been slow. A key result of Kříž is that all sets that embed in sets that are acted on transitively by a soluble group are Ramsey. We show that for nearly all known examples of Ramsey sets the converse is true, with only two possible exceptions.\n\n  * Atomically-resolved exciton emission from single defects in MoS$_2$\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2025-10-22&page=65&range=3)\nciteturn4search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: 2 days ago;   * Nearly all known Euclidean Ramsey sets are subsoluble ... Oct 20 2025 math.CO arXiv:2510.15677v3\n\n  * Nearly all known Euclidean Ramsey sets are subsoluble\n\nNatalie Behague\n\nOct 20 2025 math.CO arXiv:2510.15677v3\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2510.15677, author = {Natalie Behague}, title = {{N}early all known {E}uclidean {R}amsey sets are subsoluble}, year = {2025}, eprint = {2510.15677}, note = {arXiv:2510.15677v3} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nA finite set $X$ in a Euclidean space $\\mathbb{R}^d$ is called Ramsey if for every $k$ there exists an integer $n$ such that whenever $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is coloured with $k$ colours, there is a monochromatic copy of $X$. Graham conjectured that all spherical sets are Ramsey, but progress on this conjecture has been slow. A key result of Kříž is that all sets that embed in sets that are acted on transitively by a soluble group are Ramsey. We show that for nearly all known examples of Ramsey sets the converse is true, with only two possible exceptions.\n\n  * Atomically-resolved exciton emission from single defects in MoS$_2$\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2025-10-21&page=43&range=3)\nciteturn4search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago;   * Nearly all known Euclidean Ramsey sets are subsoluble ... Oct 20 2025 math.CO arXiv:2510.15677v3\n\n  * Nearly a